@pisceslailai/dsh-delete-risk-guard README · What it does：Anything at or above `askFromRisk` (default `low`, so every deletion under the
root) becomes an `ask` decision. Allow runs the call once; reject leaves the
files untouched.

@pisceslailai/dsh-delete-risk-guard README · The audit opinion：- a protected path, its parent, or a wildcard clear → `建议拒绝`;
- a recursive delete of an ordinary directory → `谨慎：建议先备份再决定`;
- a single file or a directory whose content is regenerable (`node_modules`,
  `dist`, `build`, `tmp`, …) → allow, with the impact stating the rebuild cost;
- a target that does not exist → allow, because nothing would change;
- a deletion whose target cannot be resolved → `无法判定，建议拒绝`, and the
  advice is to retry with an absolute path.

@pisceslailai/dsh-delete-risk-guard README · The audit opinion：- shell tools whose arguments carry `command`/`cmd` (the shipped `pwsh` tool):
  `Remove-Item`, `rm`, `ri`, `del`, `erase`, `rmdir`, `rd`, `unlink`,
  `cmd /c …`, pipeline forms such as `Get-ChildItem X | Remove-Item`,
  `[IO.File|Directory]::Delete(...)`, `git clean -f…`, and `robocopy /MIR`;
- file tools whose name declares a deletion (`delete_file`, `remove_path`, …)
  and whose arguments name a path.

@pisceslailai/dsh-delete-risk-guard README · The audit opinion：Placeholders are expanded (`%USERPROFILE%`, `$env:USERPROFILE`, `$HOME`, `~`),
relative paths resolve against the call's `workdir` or the process directory,
and `cd`/`Set-Location` earlier in the same command updates the base.
